'''Margarida Soler i Besora''' (12 April 1885 – 3 November 1958) was a Baliscan-born Balboan film director, theatre director, and studio founder. She is widely regarded as the "godmother of cinema" in [[Balboa]] for her pioneering role in establishing the territory's film industry during the [[golden era of Balboan cinema]]. Soler founded [[Estudis Costa Nord]] in 1920, the first film studio in [[Sant Cristòfor]], and directed several of the most significant films of the era, including ''[[El Camí de l'Exili (film)|El Camí de l'Exili]]'' (1923) and ''[[L'Ombra del Canal (film)|L'Ombra del Canal]]'' (1928).

== Early life and theatre career in Balisca ==

Margarida Soler i Besora was born on 12 April 1885 in [[Cerdanya]], [[Balisca]], into a prosperous middle-class family. Her father was a notary and her mother was a schoolteacher. She was educated at the Cerdanya Municipal School for Girls and later at the Cerdanya Conservatory, where she studied literature and drama.

Soler began her career in the theatre at the age of twenty, joining the Cerdanya Municipal Theatre as an assistant director in 1905. By 1910, she had become one of the most prominent theatre directors in the city, known for her productions of classical Volisanian-language drama and her championing of contemporary Baliscan playwrights. Her productions were noted for their elaborate staging and their emotional intensity, qualities that would later define her filmmaking.

Soler's theatre career flourished in the years before the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. She directed more than thirty productions at the Cerdanya Municipal Theatre between 1905 and 1918, and she was a central figure in the city's cultural life. She was also a vocal advocate for the role of women in the arts, and several of her productions featured female leads in roles that had traditionally been performed by men.

== Exile and founding of Estudis Costa Nord ==

The [[Baliscan Revolution]] of 1918 upended Soler's life and career. The revolution closed the theatres, dissolved the cultural institutions she had been part of, and placed the middle-class professionals of Cerdanya under suspicion. Soler's family was targeted — her father's notary practice was confiscated, and her brother [[Bernat Soler i Besora|Bernat]] was briefly detained by revolutionary committees. Soler herself was warned that her public profile and her association with the pre-revolution cultural establishment made her a target.

In early 1919, at the age of 33, Soler fled Balisca with her brother Bernat and a group of fellow Cerdanya intellectuals. They crossed the sea to [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Zong Balboa]], where a growing community of Baliscan exiles had begun to settle in the [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]] district. The exile community in Costa Nord was largely composed of middle-class professionals — teachers, journalists, merchants, and artists — who had lost their positions and property in the revolution and who were determined to rebuild their cultural and social life in their new home.

In 1920, at the age of 34, Soler founded [[Estudis Costa Nord]], the first film studio in Sant Cristòfor. She financed the studio's establishment with her personal savings from her theatre career, supplemented by contributions from fellow Baliscan exiles who saw the potential of cinema as a medium for documenting the exile experience and for reaching the Baliscan diaspora across the Abyalan hemisphere. The studio's facilities, built on the Costa Nord seafront, consisted of a small soundstage, a film processing laboratory, and a screening room.

Soler's decision to establish a film studio rather than returning to the theatre was driven by several factors. Cinema was a new medium in Sant Cristòfor, and the absence of established competitors allowed a new entrant to build a position quickly. Film could be exported to the Baliscan diaspora across the Abyalan hemisphere — Volisania, Tapuya, Potocsí — reaching an audience that the theatre could not. And the exile community's experience of loss and displacement provided a body of material that Soler felt compelled to document.

== Film career ==

=== ''El Camí de l'Exili'' (1923) ===

{{main|El Camí de l'Exili (film)}}

Soler's first feature film, ''El Camí de l'Exili'' (1923), was the second feature film produced in Sant Cristòfor and the first to address the [[Baliscan Revolution]] and the exile experience directly. The film was a strongly anti-revolutionary work that depicted pre-revolution Baliscan society as a golden age destroyed by the upheaval. It became one of the most popular films in the Volisanian-speaking world and established Soler's reputation as a filmmaker of significance.

The film was co-written by Soler and [[Pere Riera i Font]], a Baliscan journalist and poet. The cast was drawn primarily from the Baliscano Balboan community, including [[Lluís Vergés i Arimany]] as the family patriarch and [[Caterina Puig i Mas]] as the matriarch. Soler's brother [[Bernat Soler i Besora|Bernat]] played the eldest son.

=== ''L'Ombra del Canal'' (1928) ===

{{main|L'Ombra del Canal (film)}}

Soler's second major film, ''L'Ombra del Canal'' (1928), was a political thriller set against the backdrop of the [[Balboa Canal]]. It was the most expensive Balboan film produced to date and was notable for its ambitious set design and its use of location filming at the canal locks. The film demonstrated Soler's range as a filmmaker, moving beyond the personal drama of exile to engage with the political and economic dimensions of life in Zong Balboa.

=== Other productions ===

Under Soler's leadership, Estudis Costa Nord produced films by other directors as well as her own works. [[Ramon Vidal i Capdevila]], a fellow Baliscan exile, directed ''[[La Dona del Port (film)|La Dona del Port]]'' (1921) and ''[[L'Última Carta (film)|L'Última Carta]]'' (1930) at the studio. Soler also mentored [[Francesc Bosch i Vidal]], who worked as a cinematographer at Estudis Costa Nord before moving to [[Lumina Pel·lícules]] to direct ''[[Nits Tropicals (film)|Nits Tropicals]]'' (1925).

== Controversial films and government investigation (1930s) ==

The early 1930s marked a contentious period in Soler's career. The [[Cinema Regulation Ordinance (Zong Balboa)|Cinema Regulation Ordinance]] of 1931 imposed new regulatory requirements on the film industry, and Soler's subsequent productions became increasingly provocative. Her films of this period engaged directly with political themes — colonial labour conditions, the relationship between the Zong administration and the Baliscan exile community, and the social tensions produced by the territory's racial hierarchy — in ways that drew the attention of the Zong Balboa authorities.

In 1933, the Zong Balboa government opened an investigation into Soler's political associations. The investigation examined her contacts with [[Volisania|Volisanian]] fascist groups, who had expressed interest in her anti-revolutionary films and had sought to cultivate her as a cultural figure sympathetic to their ideology. Soler had corresponded with Volisanian cultural organisations that were aligned with the fascist regime, and she had accepted an invitation to attend a film festival in Volisania in 1932, though the visit was cut short when she became aware of the political character of the event. The investigation also examined her alleged associations with the [[Balboan revolutionary army]], a militant group that sought to overthrow the Zong Balboa administration and establish an independent Balboan state. The investigation found that Soler had no formal ties to either group, but her contacts with both — the Volisanian fascist cultural establishment on one hand and Balboan revolutionary elements on the other — were deemed politically suspect.

The investigation was closed in 1934 without charges, but it cast a shadow over Soler's reputation and contributed to a cooling of her relationship with the Zong Balboa administration. Estudis Costa Nord's productions were subject to closer scrutiny by the colonial authorities, and Soler's ability to distribute her films through official channels was curtailed.

== Great Adonian War and exile in Zong ==

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) devastated Sant Cristòfor and destroyed the film industry that Soler had helped to build. Estudis Costa Nord's facilities were damaged in the early bombing of the city, and the studio's film archive — containing the negatives and prints of most of its productions — was largely destroyed. As the siege of Sant Cristòfor intensified and the Balboa Campaign brought Volisanian and Balboan forces to the gates of the city, Soler made the decision to leave.

In 1936, Soler evacuated to [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]], travelling by naval transport to the Zong mainland. She settled in the capital, where she lived for the duration of the war. Her departure from Sant Cristòfor was motivated by the deteriorating conditions in the besieged city and by the political situation: the Volisanian fascist government, which had invaded Sant Cristòfor as part of the Balboa Campaign, had cited Soler's earlier contacts with Volisanian cultural organisations as evidence of sympathy for the fascist regime. Soler was aware that her association with Volisanian fascist groups, however tenuous, could be exploited by the occupying forces, and she chose to distance herself by relocating to Zong.

In Zong, Soler publicly denounced the Volisanian fascist government and its invasion of Sant Cristòfor. In a statement published in the Zong-language press in 1937, she repudiated her earlier contacts with Volisanian cultural organisations, stating that she had been naive in her engagement with groups that had sought to use her anti-revolutionary films for their own political purposes. She condemned the fascist regime's racial policies, its suppression of cultural freedom, and its destruction of Sant Cristòfor. The denunciation was significant — Soler was the most prominent Baliscan exile cultural figure to speak out against the fascist regime, and her statement was circulated in the Volisanian-language press in the Abyalan hemisphere, where it was read by the Baliscan diaspora.

During her years in Zong, Soler lived in modest circumstances, supported by a small pension arranged by the Zong government. She was unable to produce films, as she had no access to studio facilities or equipment, but she continued to write. She kept a diary of her wartime exile, recording her observations of life in Zong and her reflections on the destruction of the film industry she had built. She also maintained contact with the Baliscano Balboan community, many of whom had also fled Sant Cristòfor during the war.

== Return to Balboa and death ==

Soler returned to Sant Cristòfor in 1950, after the establishment of the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|International Mandate]] administration. She found the city much changed — the film industry was gone, the Costa Nord district had been heavily damaged during the war, and the surviving Baliscano Balboan community was diminished. Estudis Costa Nord's facilities had been destroyed, and the studio's surviving records were limited to the prints and documents that Soler had stored in the cellar of her Costa Nord home before her evacuation, which she recovered on her return.

Soler lived in reduced circumstances in the post-war city. She was consulted by the [[Arxiu Nacional de Cinema de Balboa]] when it was established in 1956, and she donated the surviving prints and documents she had preserved to the archive. She also donated her wartime diaries — both the diary she had kept during the siege of Sant Cristòfor before her evacuation and the diary she had kept during her exile in Zong — which together provide a detailed account of the war years from the perspective of a Baliscan exile cultural figure.

Soler died on 3 November 1958 in Sant Cristòfor, at the age of 73. She was survived by her brother Bernat, who had acted in several of her films.

== Legacy ==

Margarida Soler i Besora is widely regarded as the "godmother of cinema" in Balboa for her foundational role in establishing the territory's film industry. Her decision to found Estudis Costa Nord in 1920 — before any other studio existed in Sant Cristòfor — made her the first figure to recognise the potential of cinema as a medium for the Baliscan exile community and for the broader Volisanian-speaking world. Her films, particularly ''El Camí de l'Exili'', are among the most significant cultural artefacts of the Baliscan exile community and of the golden era of Balboan cinema.

Soler's legacy is complex. Her artistic achievements — the founding of the first studio, the direction of some of the era's most important films, and the mentoring of the next generation of Balboan filmmakers — are widely recognised. At the same time, the anti-revolutionary stance of ''El Camí de l'Exili'' and its idealised portrayal of pre-revolution Baliscan society have been the subject of critical re-evaluation, and the film is now widely regarded as a work of propaganda as well as a work of cinema. Her investigation by the Zong Balboa government in the 1930s for contacts with both Volisanian fascist groups and the Balboan revolutionary army, and her subsequent denunciation of the fascist regime during the war, have made her a figure of enduring political controversy. Scholars continue to debate the sincerity of her denunciation — whether it represented a genuine political evolution or a pragmatic response to the changing circumstances of the war — and the degree to which her earlier contacts with Volisanian fascist cultural organisations reflected genuine ideological sympathy or the naivety of an exile filmmaker seeking audiences for her work.

The [[Arxiu Nacional de Cinema de Balboa]] holds a collection of Soler's papers, including her wartime diaries — the diary she kept during the siege of Sant Cristòfor before her evacuation and the diary she kept during her exile in Zong — production notes, and correspondence with other figures of the golden era. The archive also holds the surviving prints of her films, including the only known complete copy of ''El Camí de l'Exili''.

